A||N||12||stimpy||Resetting the 'Puter||||5||
Z||000000||stimpy||04-02-2000||09:39 PM||||Finished installing my amplifier and bazooka subwoofer today.  Had to disconnect battery to wire amp, and now my check engine light is flashing like a disco light. I know that the computer needs to be reset to get this to stop, but I am a little fuzzy on how to do that. <BR>Can anyone tell me what must be done?<BR>Thanks a bunch!<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||1FieroGTRacingInc||04-02-2000||10:38 PM||||Same problem here... we need advice,theorge.... lol.. you're alway's the savior....lol   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">       1FGTRInc.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ozzy||04-02-2000||10:51 PM||||Unless I'm misunderstanding something, usually you disconnect the battery lead for a short time to reset the ECM.<P>Try disconnecting it again for at least 30 seconds...<P><BR>ogre is definatly the master though...see what he has to say.<P>Ozzy||||reg||1
Z||000003||Raydar||04-03-2000||12:33 AM||||Where did you connect the power leads for the amp? The high current (big red) wire should go straight to the battery. The "turn-on" lead should go to a switched 12v source (power antenna connection, or IGN terminal in the fuse box.) Usually the computer is reset by disconnecting the battery. <BR>Just my $.02. <BR><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 04-03-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||stimpy||04-03-2000||06:25 AM||||Yes Raydar, used 10 Ga direct to the terminal w/ inline fuse, remote turn-on to power antenna lead. Everthing hunky dorey on the install. I will try disconnecting the battery again, butisn't there something about running X minutes at XX00 RPM for engine to find "baseline"?||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||04-03-2000||04:56 PM||||Sounds like a problem......<P>The ECM should not light the SES lamp after a reset. If it has lit then a code is set. before resetting again see what the code is.<P>depending on what the new amplifiers draw, condition of batery terminals, wires, etc. you may have created an under volt problem or a current looped ground. That could set any number of weird codes. <P>This could be as simple as cleaning up ground leads, the battery wiring, etc. The thin leads of the battery are critical. These leads MUST be good or you will have problems.<P>To look for a floating ground clamp the negative lead of a multimeter to a freshly clean negative on the battery. (Yes, the battery main grounds stay attached durring this test.) Then you take the meter + lead and connect to the block and at least 2 places on the car body/frame. Sand the test point clean. If the voltage is more than 1 volt DC (+ or -) then you've likely got some grounds to clean.<P>To look for voltage drop problems touch the + test lead to the main power distribution points. You should see close to the voltage at the battery depending on just where you are testing.<P>Also have the charging system checked. could be a problem there.<P><BR>Once you've checked all the above and made any needed repairs......<P>Fiero only has an 85amp alternator. You may simply be drawing to much current. Current spike loads from the amplifier can be allot higher than most people think. A capacitor bank might help with spike current demand but you may also need a heavier alternator if this is the problem.<P>If you do install a capacitor bank then be extreamly carefull. Shorting a capacitor can generate hundreds of amps of current. Improper handling and/or installation in other ways can fry variuos other systems or cause fires.<P>You also want all the amps and caps gounded to a single point. if you need to run ground leads to do this they should be the same gauge as the main + leads. This gound point idealy should be at the block end of the main battery ground. Don't coun't on the car body to carry heacy current load reliably.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 04-03-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Standard||04-03-2000||11:48 PM||||If your car has a/c you should have a 94 amp alternator.  I'll try to find the rpo code for it.. <BR>Here's the codes<BR>K22 Generator, 94 ampere<BR>K60 Generator, 100 ampere<BR>K81 Generator, 66 ampere<BR>K99 Generator, 85 ampere<P>the codes are under the hood, next to the master cylinder.  look for a sheet of paper with a bunch of numbers/letters on it.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Standard (edited 04-03-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||04-04-2000||09:12 AM||||I have air. I have K99. <P>A 9 amp increase likely won't help much in this case anyway. This is a pretty common problem with base amps. They'll likely need a capacitor or 3 to provide the spike demand the amp needs. The alternator and battery don't really like spiky current loads. They need to check all the wiring and ground straps before adding/changing anything else.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 04-04-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||1FST2M6||04-04-2000||01:43 PM||||I've cooked 5 alternators till i had a local starter alternator shop reclock  mine. Not sure waht they did but the regulator hasn't flaked, my Autometer volt meter doesn't bounce like it udes to and I've had the same one since august! It was only $35 it will now support my Xtant and Highs amp along with 185w Low beams and all the rest of the car. I have a seperate kill switch for my computer just to kick it out of its lame safe mode that is programmed into it in case of detonation... Disconnect the battery for 30 seconds-ish and your set.. start the car drive it around.. be dissappointed.. park it where ever.. start it up again.. and POOF different car! 10 guage wire is kinda small? what kinda of power is you amp advertised to be rated at... what is it?  My Xtant uses 2 ga. and has 2, 1 farad caps. and works just fine.. the guys at the shop hate it though.. the caps are bolts of lightning waitng to zap anything it wants... make sure you discharge them befor working on ANY electrical part of the car if you have them no matter what size they are. they can kill you!    <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/in-n-out.jpg" TARGET=_blank>www.fiero.nl/uploads/in-n-out.jpg</A>   my Xtant 1001DX and Kenwood KAC-626, signaled by Sony CDX-C90. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Standard||04-04-2000||02:04 PM||||hmm.. both my cars have air and they both have k22.  I figured it was standard with a/c..||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||04-04-2000||04:45 PM||||1FST,,,,<P>The running draw of an amp is often not very much. It's the surge current when it hits a big base beat that gets you. That's why you're running the caps. Caps love to provide punch current where the battery and alternator doesn't. They like nice steady loads.<P>There's an easy way to drain the caps. disconnect the battery and put the parking lights on till the caps go flat.<P>What you don't want to do very often is just short them out to discharge them. That can mess them up.<P>If the caps have an isolation diode then you can make a safe discharge tool with an old headlamp bulb, wire and aligator clips. You just hook it up on the down stream side of the caps.||||reg||1
Z||000011||1FST2M6||04-05-2000||08:10 AM||||usually just turn on my lights... i've always had the caps... Bought them with the amp.. Amp munufacturer (not store sales rep) suggested I use 3 1 farad caps.. but i barely have room for the two i'm using... if you just short them out they can explode! and a diode takes to long... bout 4 minutes worth the juice running the lights... And even running the caps my amp still pulls 35 amps just ON! not on standby... then it's only like .003amp-hr. It's rated at 500w RMS @ 2ohms with the line conditioners running and the car in the SPL bay I can heat up the 2 guage wire in about a minute where it's uncomfortable to hold... I've upgraded it with thier bigger internal caps,line conditioner, and Low frequency quality filter (LFQ45) which is actually just a gain module... +/- 18db @ 45hz. Its VERY clear and precise... sorry for getting off the subject. ||||reg||1
Z||000012||theogre||04-05-2000||10:05 AM||||The isolation diode I mentioned isn't to drain the caps. It keeps them from back feeding the car. Not allot of systems have it. If it's not selected & installed right you blow a pretty expensive diode.(50-100 amp diodes are pricey.)||||reg||1
